to make you sick.

1811
01:11:37,960 --> 01:11:39,753
We have a movie pitch
for Jon Hamm.

1812
01:11:39,837 --> 01:11:41,839
What's it about? Lay it down
in two minutes or less.

1813
01:11:41,922 --> 01:11:43,966
Well, Terrence, I could give you
the old verbal pitch.

1814
01:11:44,049 --> 01:11:45,801
That'd be easy.
But I'm gonna do you one better

1815
01:11:45,884 --> 01:11:49,221
because sometimes, a giant
stack of loose paperwork

1816
01:11:49,305 --> 01:11:50,723
is worth a thousand pitches.

1817
01:11:52,391 --> 01:11:55,436
-Is this blood on here?
-Yeah.

1818
01:11:55,519 --> 01:11:56,562
Do you want to explain
where it came from?

1819
01:11:56,645 --> 01:11:57,479
No.

1820
01:11:58,188 --> 01:11:59,231
Okay.

1821
01:12:06,530 --> 01:12:08,574
Wow.

1822
01:12:08,657 --> 01:12:11,410
This is the kind of part
Jon Hamm would destroy.

1823
01:12:11,493 --> 01:12:13,829
Yes!

1824
01:12:13,912 --> 01:12:16,874
I think it's finally time
for you to meet Mr. Hamm.

1825
01:12:34,433 --> 01:12:36,518
Hey. I'm Jon. What's up?

1826
01:12:36,602 --> 01:12:39,646
Wow. It is such
an honor to meet you.

1827
01:12:39,730 --> 01:12:42,775
My name is Gail,
and these are my friends.

1828
01:12:42,858 --> 01:12:46,570
-Otto, Caleb, Vincent and...
-Slat-man! I owe you a text.

1829
01:12:46,653 --> 01:12:48,405
Dude, no worries at all.

1830
01:12:48,489 --> 01:12:50,491
-It's all good.
-So, my friend Terrence tells me

1831
01:12:50,574 --> 01:12:52,076
you guys have
a movie to pitch me?

1832
01:12:52,159 --> 01:12:54,411
Yes, we do,
and it is a home run.

1833
01:12:54,495 --> 01:12:56,413
Well, it better be,
'cause if it is,

1834
01:12:56,497 --> 01:12:58,499
I will literally do
anything for you.

1835
01:12:58,582 --> 01:13:00,459
- I'm gonna hold you to that.
- Okay, and, Slattery,

1836
01:13:00,542 --> 01:13:01,877
you're gonna be
co-starring in this?

1837
01:13:01,960 --> 01:13:03,253
-What?
-Don Draper, Roger Sterling,

1838
01:13:03,337 --> 01:13:04,588
back together again?

1839
01:13:04,671 --> 01:13:06,215
Yeah!

1840
01:13:06,298 --> 01:13:08,050
I'm gonna have to check
my schedule, but yeah.

1841
01:13:08,133 --> 01:13:10,260
Oh, and, Vince here
is an excellent writer.

1842
01:13:10,344 --> 01:13:11,720
He's gonna do the screenplay.

1843
01:13:11,804 --> 01:13:13,931
Oh, the all-important
scribe. Yes, sir.

1844
01:13:15,557 --> 01:13:18,268
And I assume
you are the negotiator?

1845
01:13:18,352 --> 01:13:19,895
Oh, yeah.
Well, I worked at CAA,

1846
01:13:19,978 --> 01:13:21,271
but I'm between
agencies at the moment.

1847
01:13:21,355 --> 01:13:22,981
Well, not for long,

1848
01:13:23,065 --> 01:13:24,400
if this thing does what I think
it's gonna do, am I right?

1849
01:13:24,483 --> 01:13:27,403
Yeah, he's right about that.

1850
01:13:27,486 --> 01:13:28,862
Now, what about my hair?

1851
01:13:28,946 --> 01:13:30,948
Who's gonna take care of that?
Any ideas there?

1852
01:13:31,031 --> 01:13:34,118
Um, you're looking at it.
Three years combined experience.

1853
01:13:34,201 --> 01:13:36,412
Perfect. I am psyched.

1854
01:13:36,495 --> 01:13:38,205
Let's hear this pitch.

1855
01:13:38,288 --> 01:13:40,874
Well, listen, Jonny,
I'd give the old verbal pitch.

1856
01:13:40,958 --> 01:13:42,668
That'd be easy.
But I'm gonna do you one better

1857
01:13:42,751 --> 01:13:45,254
because sometimes,
a binder full of loose paperwork

1858
01:13:45,337 --> 01:13:46,547
is worth a thousand pitches.

1859
01:13:46,630 --> 01:13:48,674
Okay, but I don't
want to read it.

1860
01:13:48,757 --> 01:13:50,884
-You...
-Just tell me the story.

1861
01:13:50,968 --> 01:13:52,511
Tell you... He wants to
hear the story.

1862
01:13:52,594 --> 01:13:54,304
- You want me to go?
- Oh, yes.

1863
01:13:54,388 --> 01:13:56,974
What do I say? Guys? Guys...
It's a story about espionage.

1864
01:13:57,057 --> 01:13:59,059
Of double identities.

1865
01:13:59,143 --> 01:14:00,519
A secret global network...

1866
01:14:00,602 --> 01:14:01,895
Okay, let me
stop you right there

1867
01:14:01,979 --> 01:14:03,522
'cause I think I get
the world of this.

1868
01:14:03,605 --> 01:14:05,357
But where's the heart?

1869
01:14:05,441 --> 01:14:07,526
-The heart?
-The heart, the heart.

1870
01:14:07,609 --> 01:14:09,695
That's where
your character comes in.

1871
01:14:09,778 --> 01:14:12,281
He's a guy who's been
put to the test...

1872
01:14:12,364 --> 01:14:14,741
-Yeah.
-Okay? Until he finally realizes

1873
01:14:14,825 --> 01:14:16,743
what it's all about.
The people you love.

1874
01:14:16,827 --> 01:14:18,203
Okay, I like that,

1875
01:14:18,287 --> 01:14:19,955
but I don't want it to be
too dumbed down.

1876
01:14:20,038 --> 01:14:22,040
Don't you worry about that.
It is the opposite of dumb.

1877
01:14:22,124 --> 01:14:23,792
It blows the lid off
the entire genre,

1878
01:14:23,876 --> 01:14:25,794
and it's all centered around
a loose pile of paperwork

1879
01:14:25,878 --> 01:14:27,713
that can't fall into
the wrong hands or else...

1880
01:14:27,796 --> 01:14:29,298
Get this, Jon.

1881
01:14:29,381 --> 01:14:31,300
-Major clusterfuck.
-Clusterfuck. That's smart.

1882
01:14:31,383 --> 01:14:33,594
Okay, so it's smart
and it has heart.

1883
01:14:33,677 --> 01:14:35,095
- Yes.
- But does it have cojones?

1884
01:14:35,179 --> 01:14:38,056
Oh, buddy, big ones.
Try this on for size.

1885
01:14:38,140 --> 01:14:42,519
One man, two fists, killing
maybe 20, maybe 30 henchmen.

1886
01:14:42,603 --> 01:14:44,354
I don't know, it felt like
the same eight guys

1887
01:14:44,438 --> 01:14:45,439
recycled over and over again.

1888
01:14:45,522 --> 01:14:47,983
But real life. Raw. Uncensored.

1889
01:14:48,066 --> 01:14:50,611
-Physical. Visceral.
-I'm getting goosebumps.

1890
01:14:51,862 --> 01:14:53,405
This is crazy.

1891
01:14:53,489 --> 01:14:54,364
- This has got balls!
- Yes!

1892
01:14:56,074 --> 01:14:57,159
Yeah.

1893
01:14:57,951 --> 01:14:58,911
Pass.

1894
01:15:00,787 --> 01:15:02,122
-What?
-What?

1895
01:15:02,206 --> 01:15:04,458
Yeah. Respectfully,
I'm gonna pass on this.

1896
01:15:04,541 --> 01:15:06,418
But thank you guys
for coming by. What fun.

1897
01:15:06,502 --> 01:15:08,170
I gotta jump
on a Zoom, so...

1898
01:15:08,253 --> 01:15:10,005
Wait, wait, wait.
That's it?

1899
01:15:10,088 --> 01:15:12,007
What don't you like? Because
we're not married to anything.

1900
01:15:12,090 --> 01:15:14,468
-Yeah, we can change anything.
-Literally everything.

1901
01:15:14,551 --> 01:15:16,970
No. Yeah, no, no,
I'm just not feeling it at all.

1902
01:15:17,054 --> 01:15:18,764
So, Terrence is gonna
show you out. Thanks again.

1903
01:15:19,890 --> 01:15:21,225
-Fun.
-Buddy...

1904
01:15:21,308 --> 01:15:22,976
Oh, hey. Y'all heard him.
Everybody out.

1905
01:15:23,060 --> 01:15:24,811
Everybody out. Let's get out.

1906
01:15:24,895 --> 01:15:27,481
You lost. It happens sometimes.
That's... That's the way it is.

1907
01:15:27,564 --> 01:15:28,732
Where's the girl?

1908
01:15:30,984 --> 01:15:32,194
What?

1909
01:15:33,237 --> 01:15:34,571
This isn't a Zoom.

1910
01:15:34,655 --> 01:15:37,157
Oh, shame on you, Jon Hamm.

1911
01:15:37,241 --> 01:15:40,285
We came all this way.
We risked our lives to find you.

1912
01:15:40,369 --> 01:15:41,662
And for what?

1913
01:15:41,745 --> 01:15:43,205
You making promises
to each of us

1914
01:15:43,288 --> 01:15:45,958
that you had
no intention of keeping.

1915
01:15:46,041 --> 01:15:48,001
You're not the person
that I thought you were.

1916
01:15:50,504 --> 01:15:51,630
You're absolutely right.

1917
01:15:54,716 --> 01:15:55,634
The honest truth is...

1918
01:15:56,468 --> 01:15:57,761
I'm just scared.

1919
01:15:59,680 --> 01:16:01,515
Sorry, Mr. Hamm.
She snuck by me...

1920
01:16:01,598 --> 01:16:03,100
Terrence, it's okay.
Please, please.

1921
01:16:03,183 --> 01:16:04,935
Don't make her sick.

1922
01:16:05,018 --> 01:16:08,981
-He's gonna make you sick, Gail.
-Guys, guys, look.

1923
01:16:09,064 --> 01:16:11,024
I made it seem like
I could set up your project

1924
01:16:11,108 --> 01:16:14,486
because that's what I thought
you wanted to hear.

1925
01:16:14,570 --> 01:16:16,989
But the truth is, you can't do
anything for any of us.

1926
01:16:17,072 --> 01:16:19,992
You're just an actor.

1927
01:16:20,075 --> 01:16:21,577
You don't know any more
about how to set up

1928
01:16:21,660 --> 01:16:23,745
the nuts and bolts of a movie
than any of us do.

1929
01:16:25,706 --> 01:16:28,584
-Isn't that right?
-Honestly, yes.

1930
01:16:28,667 --> 01:16:30,711
I'm a grown man,
but inside I'm still just a...

1931
01:16:31,503 --> 01:16:33,130
little kid.

1932
01:16:33,213 --> 01:16:36,592
That same awkward wanderer
who finally found his place

1933
01:16:36,675 --> 01:16:39,553
when he got cast as
Winnie-the-Pooh in first grade.

1934
01:16:39,636 --> 01:16:40,887
-Oh.
-My teacher said I was

1935
01:16:40,971 --> 01:16:43,265
one of the best
Poohs of the year.

1936
01:16:43,348 --> 01:16:44,850
I bet you were a great Pooh.

1937
01:16:44,933 --> 01:16:46,518
That's when I got bit
by the acting bug.

1938
01:16:46,602 --> 01:16:48,103
- Huh.
- And I never got cured.

1939
01:16:48,186 --> 01:16:49,855
In high school, I did
a wonderful production

1940
01:16:49,938 --> 01:16:51,106
-of Godspell.
-Let's get to the part

1941
01:16:51,189 --> 01:16:52,316
about Mad Men.

1942
01:16:54,151 --> 01:16:56,737
Well, Mad Men was a juggernaut
beyond my wildest dreams.

1943
01:16:56,820 --> 01:16:58,739
Of all the jobs I did in 2007,

1944
01:16:58,822 --> 01:17:01,241
it was arguably
the most high profile.

1945
01:17:01,325 --> 01:17:03,452
Those were the days.
Wall-to-wall trim.

1946
01:17:03,535 --> 01:17:04,786
But then I looked around.

1947
01:17:04,870 --> 01:17:06,038
People started
seeing me differently.

1948
01:17:06,121 --> 01:17:08,290
They started seeing "Jon Hamm,"

1949
01:17:08,373 --> 01:17:11,043
the larger-than-life guy
with the great jaw

1950
01:17:11,126 --> 01:17:13,712
and the perfect hairline.
You know?

1951
01:17:13,795 --> 01:17:15,672
So I guess I just started
reflecting back to the public

1952
01:17:15,756 --> 01:17:17,257
what I thought
they wanted to see.

1953
01:17:17,341 --> 01:17:20,969
The Jon Hamm they see on
the billboards, it's not me.

1954
01:17:21,053 --> 01:17:22,638
I'm just the guy
behind the curtain,

1955
01:17:23,764 --> 01:17:25,098
pulling the levers.

1956
01:17:25,182 --> 01:17:27,476
I guess we all came
a long way for nothing.

1957
01:17:29,186 --> 01:17:30,812
Sorry to waste your time.

1958
01:17:32,773 --> 01:17:35,359
- Let's go.
- Wait, wait.

1959
01:17:35,442 --> 01:17:37,736
I may not be able to help you
set up this project,

1960
